---

<script lang="ts">
  import DocLayout from "$lib/ui/doc/DocLayout.svelte";
  import DocHead from "$lib/ui/doc/Head.svelte";
  import DocBlock from "$lib/ui/doc/Block.svelte";
  import Code from "$lib/ui/doc/CodeBlock.svelte";
  import Example from "$lib/ui/doc/Example.svelte";
  import ConfigComponent from "$lib/ui/doc/ConfigComponent.svelte";
  import { processID, editURL } from "$lib";
  let { component }: {component: any } = $props();

  import { Badge, Alert } from "theui-svelte";
</script>

<DocLayout>
  <DocHead title="Badge" text="The Badge component shows a small count or a short label next to something else. It suits unread counts, status labels and new item markers." edit_url={editURL("badge")} />

  <DocBlock title="Example">
    <p class="not-prose">To use the Badge component in your project, import the required component first. You can create <b>dot-badges</b> just using the <code>Badge</code> component or add text inside the <code>Badge</code> element to create a text badge. Here are some examples:</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
        <Badge ariaTitle="Demo badge" />
        <Badge>New</Badge>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<script>
  import { Badge } from "theui-svelte";
</script>

<Badge ariaTitle="Demo badge" />
<Badge>New</Badge>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Grow Badge">
    <p class="not-prose">By default, the font size of the Badge component is fixed, 0.8em, making it slightly smaller than regular text. However, you can make the font size inherit from te parent element by adding the <code>grow</code> attribute to the <code>Badge</code> component.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
          <div class="w-full">
            <p class="text-2xl">NOT GROWING <Badge ariaTitle="Demo badge" /></p>
            <p class="text-2xl">GROWING <Badge ariaTitle="Demo badge" grow/></p>
          </div>
          <div class="w-full">
            <p class="text-2xl">NOT GROWING <Badge>New</Badge></p>
            <p class="text-2xl">GROWING <Badge grow>New</Badge></p>
          </div>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<!-- Indicator badge -->
<Badge ariaTitle="Demo badge" />
<!-- Text badge -->
<Badge grow>...</Badge>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Fixed Top Badge">
    <p class="not-prose">You can position badges at fixed locations relative to their parent elements using the <code>fixed</code> attribute.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
        <div class="flex items-center gap-8">
          <p class="relative p-2 bg-secondary">
            Notifications <Badge ariaTitle="3 unread" fixed class="dark:border-[#0d0d11]" />
          </p>
          <p class="relative p-2 bg-secondary">
            Notifications <Badge fixed class="dark:border-[#0d0d11]">New</Badge>
          </p>
        </div>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<p class="relative p-2 bg-secondary">
  Notifications <Badge ariaTitle="3 unread" fixed />
</p>
<p class="relative p-2 bg-secondary">
  Notifications <Badge fixed>New</Badge>
</p>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Rounded Corner">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>rounded</code> prop in the <code>Badge</code> component controls its border radius. Values: <code>none</code>, <code>sm</code>, <code>md</code>, <code>lg</code>, <code>xl</code>, <code>2xl</code>, <code>full</code>. Default is <code>full</code>.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
        <div class="flex items-center gap-8">
          <Badge rounded="xl">New</Badge>
          <Badge rounded="lg">New</Badge>
          <Badge rounded="md">New</Badge>
          <Badge rounded="sm">New</Badge>
          <Badge rounded="none">New</Badge>
        </div>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<Badge ... rounded="xl">New</Badge>
<Badge ... rounded="lg">New</Badge>
<Badge ... rounded="md">New</Badge>
<Badge ... rounded="sm">New</Badge>
<Badge ... rounded="none">New</Badge>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Customization">
    <Example>
      <p class="not-prose">To change the background color, add your own classes to the Badge. They are merged with the defaults, so a background class of yours replaces the built in one. Here's an example:</p>
      {#snippet example()}
        <Badge ariaTitle="Demo badge" class="bg-green-500"/>
        <Badge class="bg-yellow-400 text-black">New</Badge>
        <p class="relative font-semibold text-white p-2 bg-gradient-to-r from-red-400 to-yellow-500">
          Notifications <Badge class="border-white dark:border-black" fixed>New</Badge>
        </p>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<Badge ariaTitle="Demo badge" class="bg-green-500"/>

<Badge class="bg-yellow-400 text-black">New</Badge>

<p class="relative font-semibold text-white p-2 bg-gradient-to-r from-red-400 to-yellow-500">
  Notifications <Badge class="border-white dark:border-black" fixed>New</Badge>
</p>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
    <p class="not-prose mb-0">Anything else you pass reaches the element itself, so an <code>id</code>, a <code>title</code>, a <code>data-*</code> attribute or an event handler all work the way they would on a <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Accessibility">
    <p class="not-prose">Every badge carries <code>role="status"</code>, so a screen reader announces it when it appears or its text changes, which is what a count or a "New" marker is for.</p>
    <p class="not-prose">A badge with text is read as that text, so <b>leave <code>ariaTitle</code> off a badge that says something</b>. A label would replace the content, and <code>&lt;Badge&gt;New&lt;/Badge&gt;</code> with an <code>ariaTitle</code> is read as the label, never as "New".</p>
    <p class="not-prose mb-0">A dot badge shows no text, so it has nothing to be read as. Give that one an <code>ariaTitle</code> that says what it stands for, such as <code>ariaTitle="3 unread"</code>. The remaining thing to watch is contrast: a custom background needs a text color to match.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Configuration">
    <ConfigComponent {component} />
  </DocBlock>

